/LTX

/0 ""
/1 "AIRLOCK You are standing in a dimly lit airlock of a fallout shelter. You hear the faint hum of the emergency light, and an acrid smell from the fire you escaped lingers in the air.\n\nAs you look around the otherwise sterile room, you notice a small control panel on the wall to your left, next to the internal door.\n\nExits: N, S"
/2 "MAIN CORRIDOR \n\nExits: N, S, E, W, SE, SW"
/3 "CANTEEN Rows of moulded plastic chairs sit askew around bolted-down tables, their surfaces sticky with the residue of years of institutional meals. A serving hatch at the far end stands open, the kitchen beyond cold and still.\n\nExits: S, E"
/4 "BUNK ROOM Six pairs of metal bunk beds line the walls, their thin mattresses still bearing the impressions of whoever last slept here. A dog-eared paperback and a cracked photograph on one of the bedside shelves are the only signs anyone ever called this home.\n\nExits: S, E, W"
/5 "CLOSET A cramped storage closet barely wider than your shoulders, its shelves mostly stripped of whatever was once kept here. The air is stale with the smell of old wool and something faintly chemical that you can't quite place.\n\nExits: W"
/6 "LATRINE A row of lidded steel lavatories and a pair of cracked porcelain sinks occupy this narrow utility room. A persistent drip somewhere keeps time in the silence, and the overhead strip light hums with the effort of staying on.\n\nExits: N"
/7 "GYM \n\nExits: S, W"
/8 "MED BAY Banks of white-painted metal cabinets line the walls, most drawers hanging open and rifled through in evident haste. A single examination table sits beneath a surgical light that will never illuminate anything again.\n\nExits: N, S"
/9 "KITCHEN Stainless steel work surfaces run the length of this institutional kitchen, a few unwashed pots still sitting in the sink where someone left them in a hurry. The pantry to the south and storeroom to the west are a reminder of how large a crew this bunker was built to feed.\n\nExits: N, S, W"
/10 "STOREROOM Metal shelving units stretch floor to ceiling, most of them bare save for a few tins and supply boxes pushed to the back.\n\nExits: S, E"
/11 "HYDROPONICS Long hydroponic trays run beneath banks of dead overhead grow-lights, the substrate dry and neglected after what must be weeks without tending. A small bonsai tree in the corner has held on better than the rest, its gnarled trunk evidently tougher than it looks.\n\nExits: N, S, E"
/12 "PANTRY Shelving units hold an orderly supply of tinned goods and sealed containers, though several racks have been cleared at some point. A gas hose runs from a shutoff valve on the wall down to the oven, and a faint metallic smell hangs in the air.\n\nExits: N, W"
/13 "LIBRARY Floor-to-ceiling shelves hold a mix of technical manuals and battered paperbacks, a handful of volumes knocked to the floor and left where they fell. One shelf along the far wall has come away from its fittings and hangs at an awkward angle.\n\nExits: S"
/14 "LOUNGE Sagging sofas and a pair of armchairs are arranged around a low table strewn with old magazines and the scattered pieces of several abandoned board games. A television set is bolted to the wall at head height, its screen long dark.\n\nExits: N, E"
/15 "WE CORRIDOR \n\nExits: N, S, E, SE, SW"
/16 "CONTROL ROOM \n\nExits: N, S, E, W"
/17 "WORKSHOP A long workbench runs down the centre of this well-equipped room, its surface scattered with tools, coils of wire, and half-finished repairs left by whoever was last in here. A portable welding rig stands in one corner - gas tank and nozzle waiting to be connected up.\n\nExits: N, E, W"
/18 "TOOL ROOM A small utility room lined with pegboard, most of its hooks bare and empty, their tools presumably scattered to various corners of the bunker. A few items remain on the lower shelf, including a few cans of solvent that someone never got round to putting away.\n\nExits: N, E"
/19 "GENERATOR \n\nExits: N, E, W"
/20 "PUMP ROOM \n\nExits: W, D"
/21 "RESERVOIR \n\nExits: U"
/22 "ARMOURY \n\nExits: N"
/23 "OUTSIDE You step out into the wasteland. The air, though acrid, fills your lungs. In the distance a rescue vehicle is already closing in - someone heard your signal. You've won!\n\nExits: S"

/CON

